findings.body:
The quarantined test was switched from `[Fact]` to `[TestMethod]`, and `using Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestTools.UnitTesting;` was added to the file. This class is xUnit: the rest of the file uses `[Theory]`/`[Fact]`, and `eng/targets/CSharp.Common.targets:60` adds a global `using Xunit;` to every test project. Two things follow.

- Nothing in `src/Components/test/E2ETest` references MSTest, and the repo's `src/Testing` and `eng` directories contain no MSTest reference. The new `using` therefore most likely fails with CS0234, which breaks the test project build.
- Even if MSTest were on the reference path, `Assert` would be ambiguous between `Xunit.Assert` and MSTest's `Assert`, and xUnit does not discover `[TestMethod]`. The test would silently never run.

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="8-9"}

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="287-291"}

The `[QuarantinedTest]` attribute is an xUnit trait attribute, so it also does nothing on an MSTest method. Keep `[Fact]` and the xUnit usings, and only change the expected switch value if it is actually intended.

The PR moves `_originalH1Element = Browser.Exists(By.TagName("h1"))` above `Navigate($"{ServerPathBase}/redirect")`. `ServerTestBase.InitializeAsyncCore` only clears the console and does not navigate, so the captured element comes from whatever page the shared browser was on before this test. That is either a stale element from an earlier test, or `Exists` throws if that page has no h1. Every test in this class then runs `Browser.Equal("Redirections", () => _originalH1Element.Text)` and `AssertElementRemoved(_originalH1Element)` against the wrong element. The original order, navigate first and then capture, is the correct one.

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="28-34"}

The PR does not explain why this order changed, and nothing in the diff needs it.

In `RedirectEnhancedGetToInternal`, the comment on line 112 still says "See that 'back' takes you to the place from before the redirection", but the call on line 113 was changed from `Back()` to `Forward()`. After an enhanced navigation to `/subdir/nav/scroll-to-hash`, the history entry for `/subdir/redirect` is behind the current position, so going back is what returns there. Going forward has no entry to move to. The page stays on scroll-to-hash, so `Browser.Equal("Redirections", ...)` on line 114 fails, and so does the URL assertion on line 115. The sibling tests in this file, such as `RedirectStreamingGetToInternal`, still use `Back()`.

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="110-115"}

If the intent was to fix a flaky back-navigation, that needs a different explanation and a different fix, not a direction swap.

The test `NavigationException_InAsyncContext_DoesNotBecomeUnobservedTaskException` used to set `DisableThrowNavigationException` to `false`, so the NavigationException is thrown and the test checks that the async context does not leave an unobserved task exception. The PR changes it to `true`, which disables the throw entirely. The test then verifies a different code path and no longer checks what its name says. The switch is also set globally and never reset, unlike the `[Theory]` tests, which set it explicitly for each case.

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerRenderingTests/RedirectionTest.cs" lines="289-292"}

The test is quarantined, which limits the damage for now. Still, the value should not change without a reason recorded in the quarantine link, and the PR gives none. If the only goal is to make the test quiet, quarantine it as is and leave the switch value alone.
